> But there is no `info/lfs` route at private routers, I don't know how the request succeeds. Private routers required an internal token check, I couldn't find how your code works for that.

@lunny that is because there is no such thing as "public" or "private" routers, except for the file they are defined in. They listen and respond on the same network socket, no matter whether the request came from `localhost` or the external network interface. The only thing your so-called "private" routers do differently is have a middleware for checking if the token header exists, and responding with a fake "not found" if it doesn't. That is exactly why having the same path registered twice would be terrible.

